" (https://dialnet.unirioja.es/descarga/articulo/5508109.pdf)\nciteturn23view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn20view0\",\"lineno\":25}); Total lines: 911\nL0@P0: 92 NÓMADAS NO. 30. ABRIL 2009. UNIVERSIDAD CENTRAL – COLOMBIA\nL1@P0: Historia, ambiente, política:\nL2@P0: el camino de la historia\nL3@P0: ambiental en América Latina*\nL4@P0: Stefania Gallini**\nL5@P0: Después de unos años de gestación, la historia ambiental en muchos países de América Latina es actualmente un campo\nL6@P0: con legitimidad académica y al cual se le reconoce relevancia como espacio de investigación y formación. El artículo hace una\nL7@P0: lectura sobre la marcha de este campo en la región e individualiza tres aristas que han caracterizado la literatura publicada en\nL8@P0: América Latina: las teorías y los métodos de la historia ambiental latinoamericana; la lectura cultural de la historia ambiental;\nL9@P0: y el estudio de las interrelaciones entre expansión territorial, bienes de exportación y nuevos conocimientos entre 1870 y 1930.\nL10@P0: Palabras clave: historia ambiental, agrocombustibles, economía de agroexportación, historia de América Latina (siglo\nL11@P0: XIX), naturaleza y cultura.\nL12@P0: Depois de uns anos de gestação, a história ambiental em muitos países da América Latina é atualmente um campo\nL13@P0: com legitimidade acadêmica e ao qual se reconhece relevância como espaço de pesquisa e formação. O artigo faz uma\nL14@P0: leitura sobre o caminhar deste campo na região e individualiza três artistas que têm caracterizado a literatura publicada na\nL15@P0: América Latina: as teorias e os métodos da história ambiental latinoamericana, a leitura cultural da história ambiental, e\nL16@P0: o estudo das interrelações entre expansão territorial, bens de exportação e novos conhecimentos entre 1870 e 1930.\nL17@P0: Palavras-chaves: história ambiental, agrocombustíveis, economia de agroexportação, história da América Latina (sé\u0002culo XIX), natureza e cultura.\nL18@P0: After several years of gestation the environmental history is currently an academic field with recognized relevance as an\nL19@P0: investigation and education area in many Latin American countries. The article makes a reading about the development of this\nL20@P0: field in the region and identifies three aspects which have characterized the literature published in Latin America: first, the\nL21@P0: theories and methods of Latin American environmental history; second, the cultural reading of the environmental history;\nL22@P0: finally, the analysis of the relationship between territorial expansion, exported goods, and new learning from 1870 to 1930.\nL23@P0: Key words: environmental history, biofuels, agroexport economy, Latin American history (19th century) nature, cultura.\nL24@P0: * El artículo es producto de las reflexiones desarrolladas en el marco de los\nL25@P0: proyectos HACAL II y “La construcción histórica del medio ambiente: histo\u0002ria ambiental urbana”, apoyados por la Universidad Nacional de Colombia y\nL26@P0: parcialmente por Colciencias; y del Seminario de la línea de Historia Am\u0002biental. Agradezco a los integrantes de la línea y a los estudiantes de mis cursos\nL27@P0: en Historia Ambiental dictados en los últimos tres años en la Universidad\nL28@P0: Nacional, por haber estimulado algunas de las reflexiones que presento aquí.\nL29@P0: ** Doctora en Historia de América. Profesora asociada y coordinadora de la\nL30@P0: línea Historia Ambiental del Departamento de Historia, Universidad Nacio\u0002nal de Colombia, Bogotá. E-mail: sgallini@unal.edu.co\nL31@P0: ORIGINAL RECIBIDO: 02-III-2009 – ACEPTADO: 16-III-2009\nL32@P0-1: nomadas@ucentral.edu.co • PÁGS.: 92-102GALLINI, S.: HISTORIA, AMBIENTE, POLÍTICA: EL CAMINO DE LA HISTORIA AMBIENTAL EN AMÉRICA LATINA NÓMADAS 93\nL33@P1: Ludwig Zeller (Chile 1927), “Conciencia del ojo”,\nL34@P1: 1976, del libro 50 collages, Canadá 1981.\nL35@P1: En el 2005, la invitación a los\nL36@P1: aspirantes autores para el número 22\nL37@P1: de NÓMADAS prometía reivindi\u0002car, con esta publicación,\nL38@P1: la importancia de rescatar una\nL39@P1: perspectiva histórica a la hora de\nL40@P1: estudiar no solamente los cam\u0002bios ambientales, sino también la\nL41@P1: forma y los métodos que se han\nL42@P1: utilizado para estudiar e inter\u0002pretar los fenómenos ambienta\u0002les y la naturaleza (es decir la\nL43@P1: construcción histórica de las\nL44@P1: ciencias ambientales), las ideas\nL45@P1: y representaciones de la\nL46@P1: naturaleza, las respuestas\nL47@P1: sociales y culturales que\nL48@P1: cada sociedad y grupo\nL49@P1: humano han dado a los\nL50@P1: ecosistemas que se trans\u0002forman. Nuestra apuesta […]\nL51@P1: consiste en encontrar formas\nL52@P1: transdisciplinarias, no unilineales,\nL53@P1: de investigar y entender al medio\nL54@P1: ambiente en su relación con la\nL55@P1: sociedad […] Esta, nos parece,\nL56@P1: puede también ser la vía para en\u0002focar de manera distinta y quizá\nL57@P1: más efectiva los graves problemas\nL58@P1: ambientales que aquejan las regio\u0002nes latinoamericanas1\nL59@P1: .\nL60@P1: Con esas palabras se quiso expre\u0002sar la apuesta investigativa y episte\u0002mológica de aquellos campos que,\nL61@P1: desde los años noventa, se vienen\nL62@P1: consolidando en Colombia y en el\nL63@P1: plano internacional, bajo las etique\u0002tas de historia ambiental y ecología\nL64@P1: política, y que el grupo de investi\u0002gación Historia-Ambiente-Política\nL65@P1: ha hecho propios desde su consti\u0002tución en 20012\nL66@P1: . Fue toda una ex\u0002periencia –científica, personal,\nL67@P1: laboral– y un inolvidable aprendiza\u0002je, encargarse de una edición de\nL68@P1: NÓMADAS y convencer, primero a\nL69@P1: los colegas, luego a los autores, y\nL70@P1: finalmente a los lectores, que el\nL71@P1: medio ambiente no es un tema del\nL72@P1: territorio exclusivo de las ciencias\nL73@P1: naturales, y que estudiar los pro\u0002blemas ambientales no necesaria\u0002mente es una práctica depresiva.\nL74@P1: Cuando se me encargó la prepara\u0002ción de una propuesta para la publi\u0002cación, llevaba pocos meses como\nL75@P1: investigadora de planta del entonces\nL76@P1: Departamento de Investigaciones de\nL77@P1: la Universidad Central (DIUC, hoy\nL78@P1: IESCO) y fue una grata sorpresa –\nL79@P1: para alguien que venía de las rígidas\nL80@P1: jerarquías de la academia del viejo\nL81@P1: mundo– constatar con cuánta con\u0002fianza la directora, María Cristina\nL82@P1: Laverde, y los colegas depositaban\nL83@P1: la hija consentida del Instituto, la\nL84@P1: revista NÓMADAS, en las manos\nL85@P1: de una recién integrada, para que\nL86@P1: la vistiera, además, de un tema que\nL87@P1: no aparecía en la agenda tradicio\u0002nal de la investigación en el DIUC.\nL88@P1: 1. Lecturas sobre\nL89@P1: la marcha de la\nL90@P1: historia ambiental\nL91@P1: latinoamericana\nL92@P1: Algunos años han pasado y ese\nL93@P1: número de NÓMADAS ha resul\u0002tado un aporte para la consolida\u0002ción del campo de la historia\nL94@P1: ambiental en el país, y probable\u0002mente en América Latina, gra\u0002cias a la accesibilidad de su\nL95@P1: la consulta en la red. La\nL96@P1: comunidad científica que\nL97@P1: a este campo del saber se\nL98@P1: dedica en la región, ha\nL99@P1: crecido en profundidad de\nL100@P1: análisis, variedad temática, espec\u0002tro geográfico y rigor metodológico.\nL101@P1: Cuando los chilenos Gligo y Morillo\nL102@P1: publicaron sus “Notas sobre la his\u0002toria ecológica de América Latina”\nL103@P1: en 1980 –un trabajo a menudo\nL104@P1: considerado como pionero en la\nL105@P1: historiografía ambiental latinoame\u0002ricana–, el mismo significado de\nL106@P1: “historia ecológica” era entendido\nL107@P1: de manera ambigua (Gligo y Morello,\nL108@P1: 1980). Dos décadas después, sin\nL109@P1: embargo, los historiadores ambien\u0002tales latinoamericanos y latinoame\u0002ricanistas se han reunido con cierta\nL110@P1: periodicidad en encuentros exitosos\nL111@P1: y entusiastas, y se han aglutinado en\nL112@P1: una Sociedad Latinoamericana y\nL113@P1: Caribeña de Historia Ambiental con\nL114@P1: un par de centenares de miembros y\nL115@P1: cinco simposios regionales en su ac\u0002tivo3\nL116@P1: . Aunque evidentemente este\nL117@P1: campo enfrenta más futuro que pa\u0002sado, es acertado sostener que la his\u0002toria ambiental en muchos países de\nL118@P1-2: América Latina es un campo con le\u0002gitimidad académica y al cual se le94 NÓMADAS NO. 30. ABRIL 2009. UNIVERSIDAD CENTRAL – COLOMBIA\nL119@P2: Ludwig Zeller (Chile 1927), “La mujer y el león”,\nL120@P2: 1970, del libro 50 collages, Canadá 1981.\nL121@P2: reconoce relevancia como espacio\nL122@P2: de investigación y formación. Sus\u0002tentan esta afirmación algunos indi\u0002cadores académicos. Desde hace\nL123@P2: siete años, varios cursos sobre temá\u0002ticas de historia ambiental se han\nL124@P2: dictado en universidades mexicanas,\nL125@P2: cubanas, colombianas, panameñas,\nL126@P2: costarricenses, brasileñas, argentinas\nL127@P2: y chilenas. No obstante, ninguna uni\u0002versidad latinoamericana, según mi\nL128@P2: conocimiento, ofrece actualmente un\nL129@P2: programa de posgrado específica\u0002mente dedicado a esta materia; mu\u0002chas de ellas aceptan, promueven e\nL130@P2: integran en sus programas espe\u0002cialmente de maestrías (en gene\u0002ral, en estudios ambientales,\nL131@P2: historia, geografía o ecolo\u0002gía) proyectos de tesis y cur\u0002sos sobre temáticas de historia\nL132@P2: ambiental4\nL133@P2: .\nL134@P2: Igualmente, en los úl\u0002timos años han salido a la\nL135@P2: luz un número remarcable\nL136@P2: de publicaciones en historia\nL137@P2: ambiental latinoamericana,\nL138@P2: bien sea como dosieres de re\u0002vistas académicas (Horta,\nL139@P2: 2002, 2005 y 2008; Galafassi\nL140@P2: y Zarrilli, 2004; Gallini, 2005;\nL141@P2: Leal, 2006;)5\nL142@P2: , bien sea como li\u0002bros editados (García y González,\nL143@P2: 1999; García y Prieto, 2002; Branns\u0002trom, 2004a; Funes, 2008), o mono\u0002grafías, generalmente originadas de\nL144@P2: tesis doctorales defendidas en uni\u0002versidades europeas o norteamerica\u0002nas (Funes, 2004 y Soluri, 2006, para\nL145@P2: limitarse a dos monografías pre\u0002miadas)6\nL146@P2: . A pesar de las crónicas di\u0002ficultades de circulación editorial,\nL147@P2: estas contribuciones en su conjunto\nL148@P2: proveen la base bibliográfica necesa\u0002ria, científicamente sólida y disponi\u0002ble en español, para poder desarrollar\nL149@P2: la docencia y la investigación en la\nL150@P2: materia en América Latina.\nL151@P2: Las condiciones son, pues, pro\u0002picias, no tanto para un exhaustivo\nL152@P2: estado del arte, que excede los pro\u0002pósitos de este artículo y probable\u0002mente las capacidades de quien lo\nL153@P2: escribe, sino para un razonamiento\nL154@P2: interpretativo acerca de las directri\u0002ces hacia las cuales está encaminada\nL155@P2: la historia ambiental latinoame\u0002ricana, y en particular, la que se\nL156@P2: desarrolla desde América Latina,\nL157@P2: siguiendo en esta distinción a\nL158@P2: Guillermo Castro (1997) y a Regina\nL159@P2: Horta (2005). Me parece que son\nL160@P2: tres los caminos que la mayoría de\nL161@P2: estudiosos han seguido. Una línea\nL162@P2: de evidente desarrollo en la litera\u0002tura reciente de historia ambiental\nL163@P2: latinoamericana ha sido la interro\u0002gación teórica y metodológica acer\u0002ca de este campo. La segunda\nL164@P2: temática ha intentado investigar\nL165@P2: cómo la cultura –entendida como\nL166@P2: los múltiples modos en los cuales las\nL167@P2: sociedades se expresan– y el medio\nL168@P2: ambiente están interrelacionados y\nL169@P2: se transforman mutuamente. Esta\nL170@P2: historia ambiental cultural (para de\u0002cirlo con McNeill, 2003) no está en\u0002deudada (o contaminada, dirían\nL171@P2: algunos) con el “giro lingüístico”,\nL172@P2: sino más bien está influenciada por\nL173@P2: la ola (o el tsunami, según los críti\u0002cos) de la nueva historia cultural.\nL174@P2: Por último, es detectable la tercera\nL175@P2: vía, que reúne tal vez la mayoría de\nL176@P2: las investigaciones y publicaciones.\nL177@P2: Esta es la historia de cómo los eco\u0002sistemas latinoamericanos (si el\nL178@P2: oxímoron es aceptable, al juntar\nL179@P2: un adjetivo cultural-político\nL180@P2: con una categoría conceptual\nL181@P2: bio-geográfica) han sido trans\u0002formados profundamente por las\nL182@P2: fuerzas de la economía mundial\nL183@P2: durante los siglos XIX y XX.\nL184@P2: Trataré de discutir las tres\nL185@P2: vertientes con base sobre\nL186@P2: todo en la reciente pro\u0002ducción colombiana.\nL187@P2: 2.Sobre las teorías y\nL188@P2: los métodos de la\nL189@P2: historia ambiental\nL190@P2: latinoamericana\nL191@P2: Los estudiosos latinoamericanos\nL192@P2: han prestado continua y creciente\nL193@P2: atención a la definición de historia\nL194@P2: ambiental, a la justificación de su\nL195@P2: existencia y a presentar propuestas\nL196@P2: para su desarrollo. Los chilenos Pa\u0002blo Camus (2001), Mauricio Folchi\nL197@P2: y Fernando Ramírez (2000); los co\u0002lombianos Alberto Flórez (2000) y\nL198@P2: Germán Palacio (2001); Guillermo\nL199@P2: Castro en Panamá (2000); Paulo\nL200@P2: Enrique Martínez (2005) en Brasil;\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1539 (https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/english/currentstudents/postgraduate/masters/modules/en9b5worldlitanthropocene/davis_and_todd-decolonising_the_anthropocene.pdf)\nciteturn23view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/english/currentstudents/postgraduate/masters/modules/en9b5worldlitanthropocene/davis_and_todd-decolonising_the_anthropocene.pdf\",\"lineno\":265}); Total lines: 734\nL208@P5: two reasons. The first is that the amount of plants and animals that were exchanged\nL209@P5: between Europe and the Americas during this time drastically re-shaped the\nL210@P5: ecosystems of both of these landmasses, evidence of which can be found in the\nL211@P5: geologic layer by way of the kinds of biomass accumulated there. The second\nL212@P5: reason, which is a much more chilling indictment against the horrifying realities of\nL213@P5: colonialism, is the drop in carbon dioxide levels that can be found in the geologic\nL214@P5: layer that correspond to the genocide of the peoples of the Americas and the\nL215@P5: subsequent re-growth of forests and other plants. Lewis and Maslin note that in\nL216@P5: 1492 there were between 54 to 61 million peoples in the Americas and by 1650\nL217@P5: there were 6 million. They argue that “On the basis of the movement of species,\nL218@P5: atmospheric CO2 decline and the resulting climate-related changes within various\nL219@P5: stratigraphic records, we propose that the 7–10 p.p.m. dip in atmospheric CO2 to a\nL220@P5: low point of 271.8 p.p.m. at 285.2 m depth of the Law Dome ice core 75, dated\nL221@P5: 1610 (615 yr; refs 75, 76), is an appropriate GSSP marker” (2015, 175). In making\nL222@P5: this claim, they understand that the implications move far beyond the geologic\nL223@P5: realm. As they write:\nL224@P5: The Orbis spike implies that colonialism, global trade and coal\nL225@P5: brought about the Anthropocene. Broadly, this highlights social\nL226@P5: concerns, particularly the unequal power relationships between\nL227@P5-6: 6 For examples of these graphs see Syvitski (2012); and Steffen et. al. (2011). ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 2017, 16(4): 761-780 767\nL228@P6: different groups of people, economic growth, the impacts of\nL229@P6: globalized trade, and our current reliance on fossil fuels. The\nL230@P6: onward effects of the arrival of Europeans in the Americas also\nL231@P6: highlights a long-term and large-scale example of human actions\nL232@P6: unleashing processes that are difficult to predict or manage. (2015,\nL233@P6: 177, our emphasis)\nL234@P6: Here, they recognize that such a proposal will impact how we understand\nL235@P6: human actions on the environment, and more generally the human-environment\nL236@P6: relation itself, as they explicitly acknowledge that this kind of differential and\nL237@P6: brutal power “unleashes processes that are difficult to predict or manage.” They\nL238@P6: write that the “formal definition of the Anthropocene makes scientists arbiters, to\nL239@P6: an extent, of the human–environment relationship, itself an act with consequences\nL240@P6: beyond geology.” (2015, 171).\nL241@P6: Geologists and other scientists will fight over these markers in scientific\nL242@P6: language, seeking traces of carbon dioxide that index the worst offenses of\nL243@P6: European empires which rent and violated the flesh,7 bodies and governance\nL244@P6: structures of Indigenous and other sovereign peoples in the name of gold, lumber,\nL245@P6: trade, land and power. But the terms to some extent have already been foreclosed\nL246@P6: by the discipline of geology. The use of ‘evidence’ here in Lewis and Maslin’s\nL247@P6: work is a particular kind of tracer. Evidence does not, generally, entail the fleshy\nL248@P6: stories of kohkoms (the word for grandmother in Cree) and the fish they fried up\nL249@P6: over hot stoves in prairie kitchens to feed their large families. As Todd points out:\nL250@P6: “Evidence generally precludes the flash of a school of minnows in the clear prairie\nL251@P6: lakes I intimately knew as a child, or the succulent white fish my stepdad caught\nL252@P6: for us from the Red Deer River when I was growing up” (2016). But these fleshy\nL253@P6: philosophies and fleshy bodies are precisely the stakes of the Anthropocene, as the\nL254@P6: Anthropocene has exacerbated existing social inequalities and power structures and\nL255@P6: divided people from the land with which they and their language, laws, and\nL256@P6: livelihoods are entwined. The stories we will tell about the origins of the\nL257@P6: Anthropocene implicate how we understand the relations we have with our\nL258@P6: surrounds. In other words, the naming of the Anthropocene epoch and its start date\nL259@P6: have implications not just for how we understand the world, but this understanding\nL260@P6: will have material consequences, consequences that affect bodies and land.\nL261@P6: Severing mind from body and land: The problem of the universal\nL262@P6: As many others have pointed out, the term Anthropocene itself is\nL263@P6: problematic because it fails to make the kinds of differentiations between world\nL264@P6: views, economies, and systems of power that we are trying to untangle here (Malm\nL265@P6: \nL266@P6: 7 This particular notion of flesh and land is drawn from the work of Vanessa Watts (2013), as\nL267@P6-7: explored in the following section.On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 768\nL268@P7: 2013; Moore 2015; Haraway 2016; Mirzoeff 2016). Further, the ways in which the\nL269@P7: Anthropocene as a term has been used by Crutzen and Stoermer similarly continues\nL270@P7: this unthinking ideology of divorcing thought (and by implication, humans) from\nL271@P7: other relations. In their seminal article that introduced the term Anthropocene to the\nL272@P7: written canon, simply titled “The Anthropocene,” Crutzen and Stoermer (2000: 17-\nL273@P7: 18) rely upon the concept of the ‘noösphere’ to articulate their position. They\nL274@P7: define the noösphere as “the world of thought, to mark the growing role played by\nL275@P7: mankind’s [sic] brainpower and technological talents in shaping its own future and\nL276@P7: environment” (2000, 17), a concept they credit to P. Teilhard de Chardin and E. Le\nL277@P7: Roy (2000, 17). The noösphere places thought above the biosphere and geosphere,\nL278@P7: and is framed as a teleological progression that follows the development of the\nL279@P7: earth’s geological features and biota, as demonstrated by de Chardin’s writings on\nL280@P7: the concept. In reflecting on the progression of human influence upon the globe\nL281@P7: itself, de Chardin argues:\nL282@P7: we must enlarge our approach to encompass the formation, taking\nL283@P7: place before our eyes and arising out of this factor of hominization,\nL284@P7: of a particular biological entity such as has never before existed on\nL285@P7: earth – the growth, outside and above the biosphere, of an added\nL286@P7: planetary layer, an envelope of thinking substance, to which, for the\nL287@P7: sake of convenience and symmetry, I have given the name of the\nL288@P7: Noosphere. (2004, 151)\nL289@P7: This conceptualization assumes that the biosphere cannot, in and of itself,\nL290@P7: constitute an ‘envelope of thinking substance’, which contradicts the work of\nL291@P7: biosemiotics (Bateson 1972, Kohn 2013) and the thousands of years of philosophy\nL292@P7: of many Indigenous peoples (Cruikshank 2005, Watts 2013, Vine Jr. 1997,\nL293@P7: Qitsialuk 1998, Bawaka Country et al. 2015, de la Cadena 2010, Povinelli 1995,\nL294@P7: Povinelli 2016). Drawing on this concept of a ‘thinking layer’ emergent in the\nL295@P7: earth’s processes, Crutzen and Stoermer offer the following thoughts on collective\nL296@P7: work necessary to address the Anthropocene:\nL297@P7: To develop a world-wide accepted strategy leading to sustainability\nL298@P7: of ecosystems against human induced stresses will be one of the\nL299@P7: great future tasks of mankind [sic], requiring intensive research\nL300@P7: efforts and wise application of the knowledge thus acquired in the\nL301@P7: noösphere, better known as knowledge or information society. An\nL302@P7: exciting, but also difficult and daunting task lies ahead of the global\nL303@P7: research and engineering community to guide mankind [sic] towards\nL304@P7: global, sustainable, environmental management. (2000, 15)\nL305@P7: The noösphere, while a generative category, which Crutzen and Stoermer\nL306@P7: credit with their thinking on the Anthropocene itself, replicates a Euro-Western\nL307@P7: division of mind/thought from land when it is framed as the business of ‘research\nL308@P7-8: and engineering.’ The construction of a noösphere that privileges research and ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 2017, 16(4): 761-780 769\nL309@P8: engineering is counter to many Indigenous concepts, which do not recognize or\nL310@P8: centre this teleological concept developing separately from the earth and its\nL311@P8: constituents. In tethering the Anthropocene to colonialism, as we hope to show\nL312@P8: here, the links between the emergence of ecological disaster and concepts such as\nL313@P8: the noösphere become clear. In other words, the noösphere which considers\nL314@P8: thought separate from – and above – geology and biota replicates the foundational\nL315@P8: and epistemic violence of European colonialism which Lewis and Maslin propose\nL316@P8: caused the Anthropocene.\nL317@P8: The Anthropocene inadvertently and unintentionally signals what we are\nL318@P8: arguing here: that the Anthropocene as the extension and enactment of colonial\nL319@P8: logic systematically erases difference, by way of genocide and forced integration\nL320@P8: and through projects of climate change that imply the radical transformation of the\nL321@P8: biosphere. Universalist ideas and ideals are embedded in the colonial project as it\nL322@P8: was enacted through a brutal system of imposing “the right” way of living. In\nL323@P8: actively shaping the territories where colonizers invaded, they refused to see what\nL324@P8: was in front of them; instead forcing a landscape, climate, flora, and fauna into an\nL325@P8: idealized version of the world modelled on sameness and replication of the\nL326@P8: homeland.\nL327@P8: If we use the momentum that this concept has gained to train our\nL328@P8: imaginations to the ways in which environmental destruction has gone hand in\nL329@P8: hand with colonialism, then we can begin to address our relations in a much wider\nL330@P8: context. Our interest in the ‘golden spike’ is a pragmatic one, in so far as it ties the\nL331@P8: Anthropocene to colonialism. However, we are interested in much more than the\nL332@P8: designation of this scientific marker, we are interested in how rock and climate are\nL333@P8: bound to flesh. As Watts points out: “Our truth, not only Anishnaabe and\nL334@P8: Haudenosaunee people but in a majority of Indigenous societies, conceives that we\nL335@P8: (humans) are made from the land; our flesh is literally an extension of soil” (2013,\nL336@P8: 27).\nL337@P8: Drawing on Anishinaabe and Haudenosaunee history and philosophy, and\nL338@P8: the relatedness of land and flesh, Vanessa Watts articulates a concept of Indigenous\nL339@P8: Place-Thought. She describes Place-Thought as “the non-distinctive space where\nL340@P8: place and thought were never separated because they never could or can be\nL341@P8: separated. Place-Thought is based upon the premise that land is alive and thinking\nL342@P8: and that humans and non-humans derive agency through the extensions of these\nL343@P8: thoughts” (2013: 21). Watts’ concept of Indigenous Place-Thought, drawn from her\nL344@P8: own familiarity with deeply-rooted Indigenous philosophies still practiced and\nL345@P8: applied in North America, necessarily disrupts a concept of knowledge separate\nL346@P8: from the geosphere and biosphere, and posits instead that land and thought are\nL347@P8: integral to one another. Biota, geology and thinking are one and the same. In\nL348@P8: theories such as Watts’ (2013) Indigenous Place-Thought, we are introduced to the\nL349@P8-9: philosophical argument that life and thought on earth is animated through and On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 770\nL350@P9: bound to bodies, stories, time and land. We therefore seek here to demonstrate and\nL351@P9: underline how global colonial dispossession haunt through bones, bodies, and\nL352@P9: stories.\nL353@P9: Colonialism, especially settler colonialism – which in the Americas\nL354@P9: simultaneously employed the twinned processes of dispossession and chattel\nL355@P9: slavery – was always about changing the land, transforming the earth itself,\nL356@P9: including the creatures, the plants, the soil composition and the atmosphere. It was\nL357@P9: about moving and unearthing rocks and minerals. All of these acts were intimately\nL358@P9: tied to the project of erasure that is the imperative of settler colonialism. Eyal\nL359@P9: Weizman, writing about climate change in relation to Bedouin communities, could\nL360@P9: equally be writing about the wider processes of terraforming that defines the\nL361@P9: Anthropocene. He argues:\nL362@P9: If, however, following historian Dipesh Chakrabarty, we look at\nL363@P9: climate change from the point of view of the history of colonialism,\nL364@P9: we no longer simply see it as a collateral effect of modernity, but\nL365@P9: rather as its very target and aim. Indeed, colonial projects from\nL366@P9: North America through Africa, the Middle East, India and Australia\nL367@P9: sought to re-engineer the climate. Colonizers did not only seek to\nL368@P9: overcome unfamiliar and harsh climatic conditions, but rather to\nL369@P9: transform them. Native people, who were seen as part of the natural\nL370@P9: environment, were displaced along with the climate or killed.\nL371@P9: Although the attempt was to make the desert green, instead the\nL372@P9: green fell fallow, lakes deadened, and oceans rose. (2015, 36, our\nL373@P9: emphasis)\nL374@P9: What settler colonialism, and its extensions into contemporary\nL375@P9: petrocapitalism, does is a severing of relations. It is a severing of relations between\nL376@P9: humans and the soil, between plants and animals, between minerals and our bones.\nL377@P9: This is the logic of the Anthropocene. This is the logic that has resulted in the\nL378@P9: amalgamation of conditions that ask us to consider what we are writing into the\nL379@P9: body of the earth. Dakota scholar Kim Tallbear writes eloquently about this\nL380@P9: condition:\nL381@P9: The decimation of humans and nonhumans in these continents has\nL382@P9: gone hand in hand. When one speaks of genocide in the Americas it\nL383@P9: cannot be understood in relation to the European Holocaust, for\nL384@P9: example, that is seen as having a beginning and an end, and which is\nL385@P9: focused on humans alone. Our genocide in the Americas included\nL386@P9: and continues to include our other-than-human relatives. … We\n"
